  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 240 AM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Below average temps today and tomorrow with \nlingering rain chances across the south through Sat morning. \n\nThe free trial of September continues today now that the cold front \nfrom yesterday is well south of the forecast area. Some lingering \nmoisture north of the front will lead to spotty, light showers \nacross northeast NC this morning. Showers may linger along the \nAlbemarle Sound for the rest of the day as well. Otherwise, temps \nwill be rather cool for late July with highs only around 80. On top \nof that, dewpoints will be only around 60 for most of area outside \nof the southeast which will add to that early fall feel. Later \ntonight and early tomorrow morning, weak low pressure is \nexpected for form along that cold front then slide back NE. This\nwould push precip back north into the local area. The 00z suite\nof both global models and several CAMs were rather aggressive \nwith how much rain it brings into the area tonight. Did trend \nthe PoP forecast that way, but kept the Likely PoPs confined to \nthe southeast for now. The area will likely mostly dry out again\nby Sat afternoon. This round of precip should overall be pretty\nlight with minimal flood threat. Temperatures on Sat will be \npretty similar to today with highs around 80. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...After a drier start to the weak, an unsettled \npattern brings additional shower/storm chances mid week. \n\nTemperatures in the upper 80s and low 90s return Sunday and continue \nthrough next week. Sun and Mon look to be mainly dry aside from a \nstray shower/storm Mon afternoon. Storm chances return mid week as a \npotentially strong UL trough dips in over the east coast with \nmultiple sfc fronts sliding through the area. Right now the best \nchance for widespread storms would be Tues evening, but of course \nthere's still plenty of disagreement in the models on timing. With \nthe strength of trough currently depicted in all of the global \nmodels, there will be the potential for severe weather if the timing \nlines up right with the instability.",
